% A page with more pixels than a raster can be indexed by.
%
% A device reaches a pixel by its position within the raster, and the
% arithmetic that reaches a row is done in the width the interpreter
% counts pixels in. A page whose pixels outnumber what that width counts
% cannot be addressed at all: the position of a pixel in its far half is
% not a number that width holds, so it names somewhere else entirely.
%
% Such a page is refused. PLRM 8.2 has limitcheck for a limit of the
% implementation rather than of the machine, and this is one: the memory
% to hold the page is beside the point, since a device with the memory
% still could not reach the whole of it. Refusing before allocating is
% also what keeps a page nobody can draw from asking the system for the
% memory to hold it.
%
% The page size is a program's to choose, so this is reachable by any
% program, and an interpreter that ran on afterwards is the point of the
% refusal: what follows must still draw.

/failures 0 def
/fail { /failures failures 1 add store (FAIL: ) print print (\n) print flush }
bind def
/assert { exch { pop }{ fail } ifelse } bind def

% A square page whose side is under the largest length a device accepts,
% and whose area is over the count a pixel's position is held in. Both
% halves matter: a side small enough to look ordinary is what makes this
% a limit of the area rather than of the dimension.
% Asked of a device that keeps its raster as one block indexed by a
% pixel's position. A device whose page is built some other way has no
% such index and no such limit, and answers about a page this size on
% its own terms: one holding its rows as an array of strings says so by
% carrying ImgData, and one emitting a stream of drawing operators says
% so by drawing its glyphs as outlines rather than pixels.
/indexed? DEVICE /ImgData known not
          DEVICE /VectorGlyphs known not and def

/refused false def
/why null def
{ << /PageSize [65000 65000] >> setpagedevice } stopped
{ /refused true store /why $error /errorname get store } if
clear

indexed?
{
    refused (a page too large to index is refused) assert
    refused { why /limitcheck eq
              (the refusal is a limit of the implementation) assert } if
}
{
    (# this device does not index one block of pixels: a page of that size
   is its own question\n) print flush
} ifelse

% The interpreter carries on. This is asked of every device, whatever it
% made of the page above: a device that refused it spent nothing, a
% device that priced it refused before building any of it, and a device
% that accepted it drew it. None of those is a reason the next job
% cannot run, and an error a program cannot carry on from is not an
% error but a stop -- PLRM 3.11 has the error machinery exist precisely
% so a program may handle one and continue.
/drew false def
{
    << /PageSize [200 200] >> setpagedevice
    0 setgray newpath 10 10 moveto 100 100 lineto stroke
    /drew true store
} stopped { (a job after the refusal did not run) fail } if
clear
drew (a job after the refusal draws) assert

failures 0 eq { (SUCCESS) = } { (FAILURES: ) print failures = } ifelse
